WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.645
Here is how the test works.

00:00:01.725 --> 00:00:03.625
At each candidate change point,

00:00:03.705 --> 00:00:10.012
it compares the pooled transition dynamics before
and after that time using a CUSUM-style statistic.

00:00:10.092 --> 00:00:16.080
Naively plugging modern machine-learning estimators
into this comparison introduces heavy bias,

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.210
so the authors add a mean-zero augmentation term,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:22.149
producing a doubly robust estimating function.

00:00:22.229 --> 00:00:27.834
That lets flexible models like neural networks and
random forests estimate the transition

00:00:27.914 --> 00:00:31.794
and state-action distributions without spoiling the
inference.

00:00:31.874 --> 00:00:38.309
Sample splitting and cross-fitting normalize the
statistic across candidate points and test functions,

00:00:38.389 --> 00:00:44.569
and a Gaussian multiplier bootstrap approximates
its null distribution to yield the final p-value.

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:48.337
The maximum over time and test functions of the
normalized,

00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:52.170
CUSUM-weighted statistic gives the test statistic
gamma-hat.
